<title>BELGIUM: Belgian CBR 1996 group net up 12.1 pct.</title>
<headline>Belgian CBR 1996 group net up 12.1 pct.</headline>
<dateline>BRUSSELS 1997-03-12</dateline>
<text>
<p>Year to December 31, 1996</p>
<p>(in billions of Belgian francs unless stated)</p>
<p>    Group net profit		 4.355	   vs  3.866</p>
<p>    Gross dividend (francs)	95 francs, up 9.2 percent</p>
<p>adjusted for share split</p>
<p>NOTE - CBR, a unit of Germany's Heidelberger Zement, is Belgium's biggest cement maker.  </p>
<p>    Cons net income		  5.043	   vs  5.022</p>
<p>    Cons revenue		    56.352	   vs 49.795</p>
<p>Con operating income</p>
<p>    (before depreciation)	 12.564	   vs 11.208</p>
<p>    Operating profit		 7.345	   vs  6.863</p>
<p>    Cash flow			  9.624	   vs  9.781</p>
<p>    Group investments	     16.904	   vs  5.046</p>
<p>    Earnings per share	     192 francs    vs  171 francs</p>
<p>(takes account of five-for-one share split in May 1996</p>
<p>NOTE - CBR said prospects for North America and Central Europe were good for 1997, although Western European markets would rise only slightly.</p>
<p>It said Turkey would gradually increase its contribution to the group's net income, and activities in China would be pursued. Other market opening would be sought.</p>
<p>-- Brussels Newsroom +32 2 287 6810, Fax +32 2 230 7710</p>
</text>
